[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 4:40] Okay, we'll keep going. And if that changes at any point, tell me. Menu prices have not changed in two years. The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit.

[candidate silent 6s]

[candidate · 6:16] That's clarifying. Prices flat for two years, and the ticket is only up because people buy more items — so we've absorbed 40% input inflation entirely out of margin.

Quick sizing on a price increase. Today on 100 of revenue: COGS 58, labor 22, overhead 14, profit 6. If we raise prices 10% with volume held flat, revenue is 110, costs stay 94, profit is 16 — about 15% margin. A 15% increase gets revenue to 115, profit 21, roughly 18%.

The risk is volume isn't flat. At 10% price, we'd lose money if elasticity is worse than roughly minus one. Specialty coffee is usually fairly inelastic, but I'd want to test it.

Do we know anything about competitor pricing, or whether they've raised?
