[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 6:19] I don't have competitor pricing or whether they've raised. What other levers, beyond a price increase, could the client pull to reverse this?

[candidate · 7:49] No competitor data — I'll assume we're roughly in line with the market and that a mid-single-digit increase wouldn't be out of step.

Other levers, mostly on the cost side since labor and overhead are well controlled:

Procurement. Non-bean inputs are 43 points of revenue and drove most of the damage, so I'd start there — renegotiate dairy and packaging contracts using 200-store scale, consolidate suppliers, and look at private-label packaging.

Hedging. Beans are commodity-exposed; forward contracts would at least stop the bleeding being a surprise.

Waste and portioning. If spoilage or over-pouring is even a couple points of COGS, that's recoverable without touching the customer.

Mix. Push higher-margin items — the ticket is already rising from add-ons, so food and merchandise may carry better margins than milk-heavy drinks. Do we have margin by product?
